05\n```\n\nRoots: E001, E002, E003 (all start in parallel).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (artifact consumed by code) | The registry entry point builds against the comparison surface (view/route) delivered by the Dashboard comparison work; the consuming implementation is serialized on the producing one. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (API/data artifact) | The registry UI consumes the producing-run linkage exposed on model versions; the consuming implementation is serialized on the data-producing epic. |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring is blocked by every Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n| (no edge) E002 ↔ E001 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different repos/teams, no shared artifacts. The Dashboard already surfaces pipeline runs and per-run parameters/metrics via the documented KFP v2 interface, so the confirmation runs in parallel; its outcome is an input to follow-up planning, not a gate on the comparison implementation. |\n| (no edge) E003 ↔ E001 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different repos/components; run lineage capture is independent of the comparison view. |\n| (no edge) E003 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different components and repos, no shared artifacts. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9015-E001 | Side-by-side comparison of parameters and metrics for two or more selected runs in the Dashboard. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9015-E003, RHAISTRAT-9015-E004 | Split along the component boundary: run-lineage data on model versions (Model Registry) and the registry UI entry point that opens the comparison (Dashboard). Both halves are needed for the HLR. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9015-E001 **(bundled)** | Bundled, not split: the \"diff\" view is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface over the same run parameters/metrics data, in the same component and team as HLR-1. The strategy itself notes requirements were consolidated from two customer councils with overlapping wording. Splitting would build the same surface twice and force an artificial serialization edge; covered at the higher priority (P0) per the redundant-HLR consolidation rule. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9015-E005 | Documentation of the comparison workflow from both the runs list and the registry. |\n\nFull coverage: HLR-1 through HLR-4 each map to at least one epic; no orphaned\nrequirements and no epic without an HLR (E002 is the passive-component\nvalidation for Data Science Pipelines, supporting HLR-1's pipeline-run scope).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n  no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Technical Approach section. Teams were\n  inferred from the architecture-context owning-team fields\n  (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`). Epic acceptance\n  criteria were derived from the HLRs at a functional level.\n- **Affected Components is a bare list** without change descriptions or owner\n  teams. Active/passive classification was inferred: Dashboard and Model\n  Registry are active (new UI surface; new lineage data on model versions);\n  Data Science Pipelines is passive — the problem statement says parameters and\n  metrics already live in per-run Dashboard pages, and the architecture context\n  documents the KFP v2 REST API with pipeline runs surfaced in the Dashboard,\n  so no DSP code change is expected. Because the passive component is owned by\n  a different team (RHOAI Pipelines) than the nearest active epic's team, its\n  validation is a separate Implementation epic (E002) per the Step 2 exception.\n- **Redundant HLR wording**: